    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float x;\\n    float y;\\n} Point;\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    char label;\\n    int length;\\n    float * restrict xs;   // array of x coordinates\\n    float * restrict ys;   // array of y coordinates\\n} Group;\\n\\nvoid on_error() {\\n    printf(\\\"Invalid input file.\\\\n\\\");\\n    exit(1);\\n}\\n\\nint parse_number_of_groups() {\\n    int n;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" n_groups=%d \\\", &n) != 1) on_error();\\n    return n;\\n}\\n\\nPoint parse_point() {\\n    float x, y;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" (%f ,%f) \\\", &x, &y) != 2) on_error();\\n    Point point;\\n    point.x = x;\\n    point.y = y;\\n    return point;\\n}\\n\\nGroup parse_next_group() {\\n    char label; \\n    int length;\\n\\n    if (scanf(\\\" label=%c \\\", &label) != 1) on_error();\\n    if (scanf(\\\" length=%d \\\", &length) != 1) on_error();\\n\\n    Group group;\\n    group.label = label;\\n    group.length = length;\\n    group.xs = (float *)malloc(sizeof(float) * length);\\n    group.ys = (float *)malloc(sizeof(float) * length);\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {\\n        Point p = parse_point();\\n        group.xs[i] = p.x;\\n        group.ys[i] = p.y;\\n    }\\n\\n    return group;\\n}\\n\\nint parse_k() {\\n    int k;\\n    if (scanf(\\\" k=%d \\\", &k) != 1) on_error();\\n    return k;\\n}\\n\\nfloat euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(Point a, Point b) {\\n    float dx = b.x - a.x;\\n    float dy = b.y - a.y;\\n    return dx * dx + dy * dy;\\n}\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.h\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include \\\"knn.h\\\"\\n#include <arm_neon.h>\\n#include <omp.h>\\n#include <float.h>\\n\\n// Max-heap node (1-indexed)\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float dist;\\n    char label;\\n} HeapNode;\\n\\nstatic inline void heap_swap(HeapNode *a, HeapNode *b) {\\n    HeapNode t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t;\\n}\\n\\n// Sift down from index idx in a max-heap of size heap_size (1-indexed)\\nstatic inline void sift_down(HeapNode *heap, int heap_size, int idx) {\\n    while (1) {\\n        int largest = idx;\\n        int left = 2 * idx;\\n        int right = 2 * idx + 1;\\n        if (left <= heap_size && heap[left].dist > heap[largest].dist)\\n            largest = left;\\n        if (right <= heap_size && heap[right].dist > heap[largest].dist)\\n            largest = right;\\n        if (largest == idx) break;\\n        heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[largest]);\\n        idx = largest;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Insert or replace in a max-heap of capacity k.\\nstatic inline void heap_insert_or_replace(float d, char label,\\n                                          HeapNode *heap, int *heap_size_ptr, int k) {\\n    int hs = *heap_size_ptr;\\n    if (hs < k) {\\n        // Insert at end and bubble up\\n        hs++;\\n        int idx = hs;\\n        heap[idx].dist = d;\\n        heap[idx].label = label;\\n        while (idx > 1 && heap[idx / 2].dist < heap[idx].dist) {\\n            heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[idx / 2]);\\n            idx /= 2;\\n        }\\n    } else if (d < heap[1].dist) {\\n        // Replace root\\n        heap[1].dist = d;\\n        heap[1].label = label;\\n        sift_down(heap, k, 1);\\n    }\\n    *heap_size_ptr = hs;\\n}\\n\\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group *groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\\n    // Determine number of threads once (outside parallel region)\\n    int max_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\\n    if (max_threads < 1) max_threads = 1;\\n\\n    // Per-thread heaps: each thread gets a heap array of size k+1 and its current size.\\n    HeapNode **thread_heaps = (HeapNode**) malloc(max_threads * sizeof(HeapNode*));\\n    int *thread_sizes = (int*) calloc(max_threads, sizeof(int));  // persistent per-thread sizes\\n\\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\\n        thread_heaps[t] = (HeapNode*) malloc((k + 1) * sizeof(HeapNode));\\n        // Initialize with sentinel values: large distance and invalid label.\\n        for (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) {\\n            thread_heaps[t][i].dist = FLT_MAX;\\n            thread_heaps[t][i].label = -1;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    float px = to_evaluate.x;\\n    float py = to_evaluate.y;\\n\\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < n_groups; gi++) {\\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\\n        HeapNode *local_heap = thread_heaps[tid];\\n        // Use persistent per-thread size pointer so that multiple groups accumulate correctly\\n        int *local_size_ptr = &thread_sizes[tid];\\n\\n        Group g = groups[gi];\\n        float *xs = g.xs;\\n        float *ys = g.ys;\\n        int len = g.length;\\n        char label = g.label;\\n\\n        // Process points in batches of 16 using NEON SIMD (vld1q_f32_x4)\\n        int j = 0;\\n        for (; j + 15 < len; j += 16) {\\n            __builtin_prefetch(xs + j + 64);\\n            __builtin_prefetch(ys + j + 64);\\n\\n            float32x4x4_t x_vec_quad = vld1q_f32_x4(xs + j);\\n            float32x4x4_t y_vec_quad = vld1q_f32_x4(ys + j);\\n\\n            // Compute distances for 16 points (four vectors of four)\\n            float32x4_t dx0 = vsubq_f32(x_vec_quad.val[0], vdupq_n_f32(px));\\n            float32x4_t dy0 = vsubq_f32(y_vec_quad.val[0], vdupq_n_f32(py));\\n            float32x4_t dist_sq0 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx0, dx0), dy0, dy0);\\n\\n            float32x4_t dx1 = vsubq_f32(x_vec_quad.val[1], vdupq_n_f32(px));\\n            float32x4_t dy1 = vsubq_f32(y_vec_quad.val[1], vdupq_n_f32(py));\\n            float32x4_t dist_sq1 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx1, dx1), dy1, dy1);\\n\\n            float32x4_t dx2 = vsubq_f32(x_vec_quad.val[2], vdupq_n_f32(px));\\n            float32x4_t dy2 = vsubq_f32(y_vec_quad.val[2], vdupq_n_f32(py));\\n            float32x4_t dist_sq2 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx2, dx2), dy2, dy2);\\n\\n            float32x4_t dx3 = vsubq_f32(x_vec_quad.val[3], vdupq_n_f32(px));\\n            float32x4_t dy3 = vsubq_f32(y_vec_quad.val[3], vdupq_n_f32(py));\\n            float32x4_t dist_sq3 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx3, dx3), dy3, dy3);\\n\\n            // Store distances to temporary arrays for unrolled heap insertions\\n            float d0[4], d1[4], d2[4], d3[4];\\n            vst1q_f32(d0, dist_sq0);\\n            vst1q_f32(d1, dist_sq1);\\n            vst1q_f32(d2, dist_sq2);\\n            vst1q_f32(d3, dist_sq3);\\n\\n            // Unrolled heap insertions for all 16 points\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d0[0], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d0[1], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d0[2], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d0[3], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d1[0], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d1[1], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d1[2], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d1[3], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d2[0], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d2[1], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d2[2], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d2[3], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d3[0], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d3[1], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d3[2], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d3[3], label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n        }\\n\\n        // Scalar remainder (less than 16 points)\\n        for (; j < len; j++) {\\n            float dx = xs[j] - px;\\n            float dy = ys[j] - py;\\n            float d = dx*dx + dy*dy;\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d, label, local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Global max-heap to merge per-thread results\\n    HeapNode *global_heap = (HeapNode*) malloc((k + 1) * sizeof(HeapNode));\\n    for (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) {\\n        global_heap[i].dist = FLT_MAX;\\n        global_heap[i].label = -1;\\n    }\\n    int global_size = 0;\\n\\n    // Merge all per-thread heaps into the global heap\\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\\n        HeapNode *local = thread_heaps[t];\\n        int sz = thread_sizes[t];\\n        if (sz > k) sz = k;\\n        for (int i = 1; i <= sz; i++) {\\n            heap_insert_or_replace(local[i].dist, local[i].label,\\n                                   global_heap, &global_size, k);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Extract labels from the final global heap into a plain array for frequency counting.\\n    char *labels = (char*) malloc(k * sizeof(char));\\n    int actual_k = (global_size < k) ? global_size : k;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < actual_k; i++) {\\n        labels[i] = global_heap[i + 1].label;\\n    }\\n    // Fill remaining with -1 if not enough points\\n    for (int i = actual_k; i < k; i++) {\\n        labels[i] = -1;\\n    }\\n\\n    free(global_heap);\\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\\n        free(thread_heaps[t]);\\n    }\\n    free(thread_heaps);\\n    free(thread_sizes);\\n\\n    // Find most frequent label using frequency array (O(k + 256))\\n    int freq[256] = {0};\\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\\n        if (labels[i] >= 0)\\n            freq[(unsigned char)labels[i]]++;\\n    }\\n\\n    char best_label = labels[0];\\n    int best_count = freq[(unsigned char)best_label];\\n    for (int c = 1; c < 256; c++) {\\n        if (freq[c] > best_count) {\\n            best_count = freq[c];\\n            best_label = (char)c;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    free(labels);\\n    return best_label;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\\n\\n    Group *groups = (Group *)malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; ++i) {\\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\\n    }\\n\\n    int k = parse_k();\\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%c\\\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\\n}\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.c\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Integrating SoA storage with restrict qualifiers (B), software prefetching before SIMD batches (B), max-heap top-k selection and frequency-array final label counting (A), and OpenMP parallelization over groups with persistent per-thread heaps allocated once outside the parallel region (C) will yield a correct program that is faster than any single transformation by combining memory-friendly layout, reduced cache misses via prefetching, efficient streaming heap operations, and thread-level parallelism without repeated allocation overhead.\",\n  \"notes\": \"Added restrict qualifiers to xs/ys pointers in Group struct (B). Added __builtin_prefetch calls before each batch of 16 points in the SIMD loop (B). Retained existing SoA layout (xs/ys arrays), NEON SIMD with vld1q_f32_x4, max-heap insertion/replacement logic from A and B, per-thread heaps allocated once outside parallel region from C, frequency-array final label selection from A. The OpenMP schedule remains static over groups as in the parent commit.\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [\"11cd5b5226c14303\", \"d19340a2f7504842\", \"89c5107aac544c1f\"],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Add restrict qualifiers to Group pointer members (xs, ys) for better aliasing information\",\n    \"Insert software prefetching before each SIMD batch of 16 points\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We are in the integration role (G). We have three source ideas to integrate: 11cd5b5226c14303, d19340a2f7504842, 89c5107aac544c1f. The current code already integrates many features from these sources: SoA storage with xs/ys arrays (from B), NEON SIMD batch of 16 using vld1q_f32_x4 (B's idea but we used x4 instead of single loads), max-heap top-k selection (A and B both had heap approaches, current uses a global merge after per-thread heaps from C). The OpenMP parallelization over groups with per-thread heaps is from C. However, the sources_kept must be exactly those three IDs.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate that integrates all three transformations. Let's review what each source contributed:\n\n- Source A (11cd5b5226c14303): Interleave x and y coordinates into a single coords array instead of two separate arrays; adjust distance computation accordingly. Also uses max-heap with sift_down/sift_up, frequency array for final label selection.\n\n- Source B (d19340a2f7504842): Hoist invariant point coordinates outside inner loop, add software prefetching for xs/ys arrays, add restrict qualifiers to Group pointer members. Uses NEON SIMD but with vld1q_f32 (single vector) and batch of 4.\n\n- Source C (89c5107aac544c1f): Flatten group-point iteration into single loop over all points, OpenMP parallel for with static scheduling on flattened index, per-thread local top-k arrays allocated once outside parallel region, early skip if distance not better than kth best, linear merge of per-thread lists preserving order by thread ID.\n\nCurrent code already has many aspects: SoA (xs/ys), NEON batch 16, max-heap with sift_down/sift_up, OpenMP over groups with per-thread heaps and persistent sizes. But we are missing some specific transformations from each source that could be beneficial:\n\nFrom A: interleaved coords array instead of separate xs/ys? That would change the memory layout to a single float array where x0,y0,x1,y1,... This might improve cache locality for distance computation because both coordinates are adjacent. However, current code uses two separate arrays (xs and ys) which is also fine but interleaving could be better. Also A's frequency counting approach using freq[256] is already used.\n\nFrom B: hoist invariant point coordinates outside inner loop (already done with px, py). Software prefetching for xs/ys arrays? We can add __builtin_prefetch calls before the batch loops to reduce cache misses. Restrict qualifiers on Group pointer members could help compiler optimizations by indicating no aliasing.\n\nFrom C: flatten group-point iteration into single loop over all points (instead of nested groups then points). This might allow better load balancing because static scheduling on a flat index distributes work more evenly than per-group scheduling if groups have varying sizes. Also early skip if distance not better than kth best could reduce heap operations. Linear merge preserving order by thread ID is already done via global heap insertion (which doesn't preserve original point order but that's fine for correctness as long as tie-breaking matches baseline? Actually the baseline uses qsort on labels after sorting distances, so ties are broken arbitrarily based on sort stability? The problem statement says \"the k points with the smallest distance values\" and then frequency. If there are ties in distance, any selection among tied distances is acceptable because the output only depends on frequencies of labels among those k points. However, if two different sets of k nearest neighbors (with same distances but different label composition) could yield different majority? In theory yes, but the baseline's behavior with qsort might be deterministic based on input order? Actually the original code uses insertion sort that preserves order for equal distances because it only inserts when d < distances[x] or distances[x]==-1. So if two points have same distance, the first one encountered will stay in lower index (since condition is strict less). That means ties are broken by encounter order: earlier point wins. Our heap approach with max-heap does not preserve that tie-breaking because we replace root only when d < heap[1].dist, so equal distances do not replace. So if there are multiple points with same distance as the kth best, our selection might differ from baseline. However, in previous experiments this was correct (score >0.98) meaning it matched output for given test cases? Possibly because ties were rare or didn't affect majority. But to be safe, we could adopt C's approach of linear merge preserving original order by thread ID and using tie-breaking by lower thread ID (which approximates original encounter order if static scheduling preserves global order). However, current code uses a global heap that doesn't preserve any ordering for equal distances; it just keeps the first k distinct distances? Actually max-heap with strict less replacement: if d == heap[1].dist, we don't replace. So among points with same distance as the worst in heap, only those encountered before filling the heap are kept. This might differ from baseline's insertion sort which would keep earlier ones for equal distances. But since previous integrations were correct, maybe it's fine.\n\nGiven that our current code already has many features and is fast (~0.028s), we can try to integrate missing transformations: interleaved coords (A), prefetching and restrict (B), flatten loop with early skip (C). However, note that the current code uses per-thread heaps allocated once outside parallel region (from C) but still iterates over groups in OpenMP for. Flattening would require building a flat array of all points first (like C did) which adds memory overhead and copying time. But it might improve load balancing if group sizes vary widely. Also early skip could reduce heap operations.\n\nGiven the deadline, we should propose one candidate that combines these transformations while preserving correctness. We'll modify src/knn.c to:\n\n- Change Group struct in knn.h? No, we cannot change tests or input/output contract. But we can keep SoA as is (xs/ys) because interleaving would require changing the parsing and group structure which might break compatibility with other experiments. Actually source A's transformation was applied on top of original code that used Point arrays; they changed to a single coords array for all points after flattening? Wait, looking at diff: In source A, they allocated coords as float* of size 2*total_points and copied xs/ys into interleaved. That is done inside knn() after parsing groups (which still have separate xs/ys). So we can do that without changing Group struct. We'll add a flatten step to create an interleaved array for all points, then process with SIMD on that interleaved array? But current NEON code uses two separate arrays (xs and ys) which is fine; interleaving might allow loading both coordinates in one load but we already have them separate. Actually vld1q_f32_x4 loads 16 floats from a single pointer, so if we had interleaved coords, we could load x0,y0,x1,y1,... and then deinterlace? That would be more complex. So maybe keep xs/ys as is.\n\n- Add software prefetching: before the batch loop, __builtin_prefetch(xs + j + 16) etc. Also add restrict qualifiers to Group pointer members in knn.h (but we cannot change header? Actually we can modify src/knn.h because it's part of source files. The instruction says \"Edit only source files under src/.\" So we can edit both knn.c and knn.h.\n\n- Flatten the iteration: Instead of parallel for over groups, compute total_points, allocate arrays to store label and point (or just use interleaved coords with labels). Then parallel for over flattened index. This would require copying all points into a flat array first (like C did). That adds overhead but may improve load balancing.\n\n- Early skip: In the inner loop, before heap insertion, check if distance is >= current kth best in local heap? But we have max-heap so worst is at root; we can compare d < heap[1].dist to decide whether to insert. Actually our heap_insert_or_replace already does that when heap_size == k: it only replaces if d < heap[1].dist. So early skip is implicit.\n\n- Preserve tie-breaking? We could use linear merge like C did, but current global heap merge might be fine.\n\nGiven the complexity and risk of breaking correctness (previous incorrect attempts), we should aim for a conservative integration that combines all three sources' key ideas without introducing new bugs. The most impactful missing pieces are: prefetching from B, restrict qualifiers from B, flatten loop with static scheduling on flat index from C, and interleaved coords from A? But interleaving might not be beneficial given we already have SIMD on separate arrays.\n\nLet's examine the current code more carefully. It uses per-thread heaps allocated once outside parallel region (from C). The OpenMP loop is over groups with static scheduling. This could cause load imbalance if group sizes vary. Flattening would help but adds memory overhead and copy time. However, previous integration e5cacea750d24c29 used this approach and was correct and fast (~0.028s). So it's already good.\n\nWe need to propose a new candidate that integrates all three sources explicitly. The transformations we will apply:\n\n1. From A: Use interleaved coords array for distance computation (but keep xs/ys in Group struct, flatten into single array inside knn). This changes the SIMD loop to load from interleaved array? Actually if we have interleaved coords, we can still use vld2q_f32 to deinterlace two vectors of x and y. That might be more efficient than separate loads. But current code uses vld1q_f32_x4 which loads 16 floats from a single pointer; for xs it's fine, but for interleaved we would need different approach. Simpler: keep xs/ys as is.\n\n2. From B: Add __builtin_prefetch before batch loops and add restrict qualifiers to Group members in knn.h (xs and ys pointers). Also hoist invariant point coordinates already done.\n\n3. From C: Flatten the iteration into a single loop over all points, using static scheduling on flat index. This requires building arrays of labels and either interleaved coords or separate xs/ys? We'll build an array of Point structs (or just store label and use original groups' xs/ys via indexing). But to avoid copying coordinates again, we can create a flat array of indices into the group points? That would be complex. Simpler: follow C's approach exactly: allocate LabeledPoint array with label and Point p for each point. Then parallel for over that array. This adds memory overhead but is straightforward.\n\nGiven time constraints, I'll propose an integration that:\n\n- Modifies knn.h to add restrict qualifiers on xs and ys pointers in Group struct.\n- In knn.c:\n   - Flatten all points into a single array of (label, Point) as C did.\n   - Use OpenMP parallel for with static scheduling over flattened index.\n   - Per-thread heaps allocated once outside parallel region (already done).\n   - Inside loop: compute distance using euclidean_distance_no_sqrt? But we can inline SIMD? Since points are stored as separate x,y in Point struct, we cannot easily use NEON on that because they are not contiguous. So we would lose SIMD benefit if we flatten into Point array. To retain SIMD, we could instead flatten into interleaved coords (like A) and labels arrays, then process with SIMD batches of 16 from the interleaved array using vld2q_f32 to get x and y vectors? That would be more efficient.\n\nActually source C's approach did not use SIMD; it used scalar distance. But we want to combine all three: SoA (B) already gives xs/ys arrays per group, but if we flatten into a single array of points, we lose the contiguous xs/ys for each point because they are interleaved in memory? Wait, if we create an array of Point structs, then x and y are adjacent within each struct but not across points. That is AoS layout. To use SIMD efficiently, we need SoA (separate arrays) or at least contiguous xs and ys. The current code uses per-group xs/ys which are contiguous for that group. If we flatten into a single array of all points as Point structs, we lose the ability to load 16 x's in one vector because they are not consecutive in memory (each point has x,y). So SIMD would be less effective.\n\nThus, better approach: Keep per-group xs/ys arrays but parallelize over groups with static scheduling? That is what current code does. To improve load balancing, we could use dynamic or guided schedule instead of static. But source C used flattened index to get good load balance. However, if group sizes vary widely, static on groups may be imbalanced. We can change schedule(static) to schedule(dynamic) or schedule(guided). That is a simple transformation from C? Actually C used static on flat index; but we could use dynamic on groups.\n\nGiven the complexity and risk of breaking correctness (previous incorrect attempts due to tie-breaking issues), I think it's safer to keep the current structure that already works well (~0.028s) and add prefetching, restrict qualifiers, and maybe interleaved coords for distance computation within each group? But we can also flatten into a single array of xs and ys (two separate arrays) across all groups, then process with SIMD on those global arrays. That would combine A's idea of having one big array but keep SoA.\n\nLet's design:\n\n- In knn(), first count total_points.\n- Allocate two float arrays: all_xs = malloc(total_points * sizeof(float)), all_ys similarly, and a char* labels_all.\n- Copy data from groups into these flat arrays (preserving order).\n- Then parallel for over flattened index with static scheduling. Each thread processes its chunk of the flat array using SIMD batches on the global xs/ys arrays. This gives good load balance and retains SIMD efficiency.\n\nThis is essentially combining A's flattening idea (single coords array) but keeping separate x and y arrays instead of interleaved. We'll also add prefetching before each batch.\n\nWe need to ensure correctness: tie-breaking for equal distances? The heap insertion uses strict less, so if two points have same distance as the kth best, only those encountered first (lower index in flat array) will be kept because once heap is full, we only replace when d < root. So ties are broken by encounter order (earlier point wins). This matches baseline's behavior? Baseline insertion sort also uses strict less and preserves earlier points for equal distances. So it should match.\n\nWe'll keep the per-thread heaps allocated outside parallel region as before.\n\nThus, transformations:\n\n- Flatten all group data into global arrays of xs, ys, labels (from A and C).\n- Use OpenMP parallel for with static scheduling over flattened index (C).\n- Process in batches of 16 using NEON SIMD on the flat xs/ys arrays (B's idea but adapted to flat arrays).\n- Add __builtin_prefetch before each batch (B).\n- Add restrict qualifiers to Group members in knn.h (B).\n- Use frequency array for final label selection (A).\n\nWe'll also keep the max-heap structure from A and B.\n\nNow, produce the code. We need to output complete contents of changed files: src/knn.c and src/knn.h.\n\nLet's write the new knn.c:\n\n```c\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#include <arm_neon.h>\n#include <omp.h>\n#include <float.h>\n\n// Max-heap node (1-indexed)\ntypedef struct {\n    float dist;\n    char label;\n} HeapNode;\n\nstatic inline void heap_swap(HeapNode *a, HeapNode *b) {\n    HeapNode t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t;\n}\n\nstatic inline void sift_down(HeapNode *heap, int heap_size, int idx) {\n    while (1) {\n        int largest = idx;\n        int left = 2 * idx;\n        int right = 2 * idx + 1;\n        if (left <= heap_size && heap[left].dist > heap[largest].dist)\n            largest = left;\n        if (right <= heap_size && heap[right].dist > heap[largest].dist)\n            largest = right;\n        if (largest == idx) break;\n        heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[largest]);\n        idx = largest;\n    }\n}\n\nstatic inline void heap_insert_or_replace(float d, char label,\n                                          HeapNode *heap, int *heap_size_ptr, int k) {\n    int hs = *heap_size_ptr;\n    if (hs < k) {\n        hs++;\n        int idx = hs;\n        heap[idx].dist = d;\n        heap[idx].label = label;\n        while (idx > 1 && heap[idx / 2].dist < heap[idx].dist) {\n            heap_swap(&heap[idx], &heap[idx / 2]);\n            idx /= 2;\n        }\n    } else if (d < heap[1].dist) {\n        heap[1].dist = d;\n        heap[1].label = label;\n        sift_down(heap, k, 1);\n    }\n    *heap_size_ptr = hs;\n}\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group *groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    // Count total points\n    long total_points = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n        total_points += groups[i].length;\n    }\n\n    // Flatten all group data into global arrays: xs, ys, labels\n    float *all_xs = (float *)malloc(total_points * sizeof(float));\n    float *all_ys = (float *)malloc(total_points * sizeof(float));\n    char  *all_labels = (char *)malloc(total_points * sizeof(char));\n\n    long idx = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n        Group g = groups[i];\n        int len = g.length;\n        float *xs = g.xs;\n        float *ys = g.ys;\n        char label = g.label;\n        for (int j = 0; j < len; j++) {\n            all_xs[idx] = xs[j];\n            all_ys[idx] = ys[j];\n            all_labels[idx] = label;\n            idx++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Determine number of threads\n    int max_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    if (max_threads < 1) max_threads = 1;\n\n    // Per-thread heaps\n    HeapNode **thread_heaps = (HeapNode**) malloc(max_threads * sizeof(HeapNode*));\n    int *thread_sizes = (int*) calloc(max_threads, sizeof(int));\n\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\n        thread_heaps[t] = (HeapNode*) malloc((k + 1) * sizeof(HeapNode));\n        for (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) {\n            thread_heaps[t][i].dist = FLT_MAX;\n            thread_heaps[t][i].label = -1;\n        }\n    }\n\n    float px = to_evaluate.x;\n    float py = to_evaluate.y;\n\n    // Parallel loop over flattened points with static scheduling\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (long i = 0; i < total_points; i++) {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        HeapNode *local_heap = thread_heaps[tid];\n        int *local_size_ptr = &thread_sizes[tid];\n\n        // Process in batches of 16 using NEON SIMD\n        long j = i;\n        if (j + 15 < total_points) {\n            __builtin_prefetch(all_xs + j + 32);\n            __builtin_prefetch(all_ys + j + 32);\n\n            float32x4_t x0 = vld1q_f32(all_xs + j);\n            float32x4_t y0 = vld1q_f32(all_ys + j);\n            float32x4_t dx0 = vsubq_f32(x0, vdupq_n_f32(px));\n            float32x4_t dy0 = vsubq_f32(y0, vdupq_n_f32(py));\n            float32x4_t d0 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx0, dx0), dy0, dy0);\n\n            float32x4_t x1 = vld1q_f32(all_xs + j + 4);\n            float32x4_t y1 = vld1q_f32(all_ys + j + 4);\n            float32x4_t dx1 = vsubq_f32(x1, vdupq_n_f32(px));\n            float32x4_t dy1 = vsubq_f32(y1, vdupq_n_f32(py));\n            float32x4_t d1 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx1, dx1), dy1, dy1);\n\n            float32x4_t x2 = vld1q_f32(all_xs + j + 8);\n            float32x4_t y2 = vld1q_f32(all_ys + j + 8);\n            float32x4_t dx2 = vsubq_f32(x2, vdupq_n_f32(px));\n            float32x4_t dy2 = vsubq_f32(y2, vdupq_n_f32(py));\n            float32x4_t d2 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx2, dx2), dy2, dy2);\n\n            float32x4_t x3 = vld1q_f32(all_xs + j + 12);\n            float32x4_t y3 = vld1q_f32(all_ys + j + 12);\n            float32x4_t dx3 = vsubq_f32(x3, vdupq_n_f32(px));\n            float32x4_t dy3 = vsubq_f32(y3, vdupq_n_f32(py));\n            float32x4_t d3 = vmlaq_f32(vmulq_f32(dx3, dx3), dy3, dy3);\n\n            // Store distances\n            float dists[16];\n            vst1q_f32(dists, d0);\n            vst1q_f32(dists+4, d1);\n            vst1q_f32(dists+8, d2);\n            vst1q_f32(dists+12, d3);\n\n            // Unrolled heap insertions for 16 points\n            #pragma GCC unroll 16\n            for (int t = 0; t < 16; t++) {\n                heap_insert_or_replace(dists[t], all_labels[j + t],\n                                       local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\n            }\n            i += 15; // loop increment will add 1 more to skip processed points\n        } else {\n            // Scalar remainder for last chunk (less than 16)\n            float dx = all_xs[i] - px;\n            float dy = all_ys[i] - py;\n            float d = dx*dx + dy*dy;\n            heap_insert_or_replace(d, all_labels[i], local_heap, local_size_ptr, k);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Global merge\n    HeapNode *global_heap = (HeapNode*) malloc((k + 1) * sizeof(HeapNode));\n    for (int i = 1; i <= k; i++) {\n        global_heap[i].dist = FLT_MAX;\n        global_heap[i].label = -1;\n    }\n    int global_size = 0;\n\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\n        HeapNode *local = thread_heaps[t];\n        int sz = thread_sizes[t];\n        if (sz > k) sz = k;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= sz; i++) {\n            heap_insert_or_replace(local[i].dist, local[i].label,\n                                   global_heap, &global_size, k);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Extract labels\n    char *labels = (char*) malloc(k * sizeof(char));\n    int actual_k = (global_size < k) ? global_size : k;\n    for (int i = 0; i < actual_k; i++) {\n        labels[i] = global_heap[i + 1].label;\n    }\n    for (int i = actual_k; i < k; i++) {\n        labels[i] = -1;\n    }\n\n    free(global_heap);\n    for (int t = 0; t < max_threads; t++) {\n        free(thread_heaps[t]);\n    }\n    free(thread_heaps);\n    free(thread_sizes);\n\n    // Frequency counting\n    int freq[256] = {0};\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n        if (labels[i] >= 0)\n            freq[(unsigned char)labels[i]]++;\n    }\n\n    char best_label = labels[0];\n    int best_count = freq[(unsigned char)best_label];\n    for (int c = 1; c < 256; c++) {\n        if (freq[c] > best_count) {\n            best_count = freq[c];\n            best_label = (char)c;\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(labels);\n    free(all_xs);\n    free(all_ys);\n    free(all_labels);\n\n    return best_label;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\n\n    Group *groups = (Group *)malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; ++i) {\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\n    }\n\n    int k = parse_k();\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\n\n    printf(\"%c\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\n}\n```\n\nBut there is a problem: the parallel loop uses `for (long i = 0; i < total_points; i++)` and inside we try to process batches of 16 by incrementing i manually. This will cause incorrect iteration because OpenMP distributes iterations among threads based on original loop index, but if we modify i inside the loop body, it can lead to undefined behavior (OpenMP does not allow modifying the loop counter). We need a different approach: either use a while loop with atomic or critical sections? Better to keep the per-group parallelization as before, which already works. The flattening idea is good but implementing SIMD batches inside OpenMP for loop requires careful handling.\n\nGiven time constraints and risk of bugs, I'll revert to the previous working structure (parallel over groups) but add prefetching and restrict qualifiers. That will be a safe integration that includes transformations from all three sources: A's heap and frequency counting already present; B's prefetching and restrict; C's per-thread heaps allocated once outside parallel region (already done). We'll also change schedule to dynamic for better load balancing? But source C used static on flat index, not dynamic. To be faithful, we can keep static.\n\nThus final plan:\n\n- In knn.h: add `restrict` qualifiers to xs and ys pointers in Group struct.\n- In knn.c:\n   - Add __builtin_prefetch before the batch loop (for both xs and ys).\n   - Keep everything else as current code (which already has A's heap, B's SIMD, C's per-thread heaps).\n\nThis is a minimal change that integrates all three sources' transformations without breaking correctness.\n\nLet's produce final files."
